  "live_records": {
    "q-134399": {
      "dom_id": "step-q134399",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{2,\\,1,\\,-1\\},\\,y=\\{15,\\,12,\\,9\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{2}{3},$ $\\overset{―}{y}=12,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}≈1.247,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}≈2.449.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and ${\\sigma }_{x}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(2-\\frac{2}{3})(15-12)+(1-\\frac{2}{3})(12-12)+(-1-\\frac{2}{3})(9-12)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{4}{3})(3)+(\\frac{1}{3})(0)+(-\\frac{5}{3})(-3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[4+0+5] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{3}{1.247\\cdot 2.449} \\\\  & ≈0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
