    "q-134393": {
      "dom_id": "step-q134393",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ such that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=-8$ and their standard deviations are ${\\sigma }_{x}=3$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=4.$ Compute the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our numbers, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-8)}{3\\cdot 4} \\\\  & ≈-0.67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
