      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{13,\\,4,\\,10\\},\\,y=\\{0,\\,-3,\\,9\\}\n$$\n\nYou are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=9$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=2.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(13-9)(0-2)+(4-9)(-3-2)+(10-9)(9-2) \\\\  & =(4)(-2)+(-5)(-5)+(1)(7) \\\\  & =-8+25+7 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(13-9)^{2}+(4-9)^{2}+(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =16+25+1 \\\\  & =42 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(-3-2)^{2}+(9-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+25+49 \\\\  & =78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{42}\\cdot \\sqrt{78}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{24}{6.480\\,741\\cdot 8.831\\,761} \\\\  & ≈0.42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
