    "q-134412": {
      "dom_id": "step-q134412",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "A teacher of a group of $4$ students wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x,$ the number of hours the students had slept the night before an exam, and $y,$ the student's score in the exam. The data is shown below. \n\n| **Number of hours** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Score ** | $56$ | $68$ | $81$ | $79$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=71.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈0.81$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*",
      "worked_solution": "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. To calculate $\\rho ,$ we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(2-5)(56-71)+(4-5)(68-71) \\\\  & +(6-5)(81-71)+(8-5)(79-71) \\\\  & =(-3)(-15)+(-1)(-3)+(1)(10)+(3)(8) \\\\  & =45+3+10+24 \\\\  & =82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(2-5)^{2}+(4-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2}+(8-5)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(56-71)^{2}+(68-71)^{2}+(81-71)^{2}+(79-71)^{2} \\\\  & =225+9+100+64 \\\\  & =398\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{82}{\\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{398}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{82}{4.472\\,136\\cdot 19.949\\,937} \\\\  & ≈0.92,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
