    "q-134360": {
      "dom_id": "step-q134360",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Mark collected information about the weather in his city every day during a particular year. He wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x,$ the temperature, in Celsius degrees, and\n\n- $y,$ the relative humidity, expressed as a percentage.\n\nYou are given that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=75,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}=14,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=15.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈0.44$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Calculating the correlation coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{75}{14\\cdot 15} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{14} \\\\  & ≈0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
